<article class="env-lemma" id="0AYM">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AYM">Lemma <span data-tag="0AYM">31.16.12</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Noetherian scheme which has an ample invertible sheaf. Then every invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module is isomorphic to </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathcal{O}_ X(D - D') = \mathcal{O}_ X(D) \otimes _{\mathcal{O}_ X} \mathcal{O}_ X(D')^{\otimes -1}  \]
</div>
  <p> for some effective Cartier divisors $D, D'$ in $X$. Moreover, given a finite subset $E \subset X$ we may choose $D, D'$ such that $E \cap D = \emptyset $ and $E \cap D' = \emptyset $. If $X$ is quasi-affine, then we may choose $D' = \emptyset $. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $x_1, \ldots , x_ n$ be the associated points of $X$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/05AF" data-tag="05AF">31.2.5</a>). 
    <p>
      If $X$ is quasi-affine and $\mathcal{N}$ is any invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module, then we can pick a section $t$ of $\mathcal{N}$ which does not vanish at any of the points of $E \cup \{ x_1, \ldots , x_ n\} $, see Properties, Lemma <a href="/tag/0F20" data-tag="0F20">28.30.7</a>. Then $t$ is a regular section of $\mathcal{N}$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0AYL" data-tag="0AYL">31.16.1</a>. Hence $\mathcal{N} \cong \mathcal{O}_ X(D)$ where $D = Z(t)$ is the effective Cartier divisor corresponding to $t$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01X0" data-tag="01X0">31.15.10</a>. Since $E \cap D = \emptyset $ by construction we are done in this case. 
    <p>
      Returning to the general case, let $\mathcal{L}$ be an ample invertible sheaf on $X$. There exists an $m > 0$ and a section $s \in \Gamma (X, \mathcal{L}^{\otimes m})$ such that $X_ s$ is affine and such that $E \cup \{ x_1, \ldots , x_ n\}  \subset X_ s$ (Properties, Lemma <a href="/tag/09NV" data-tag="09NV">28.30.6</a>). 
    <p>
      Let $\mathcal{N}$ be an arbitrary invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. By the quasi-affine case, we can find a section $t \in \mathcal{N}(X_ s)$ which does not vanish at any point of $E \cup \{ x_1, \ldots , x_ n\} $. By Properties, Lemma <a href="/tag/01PW" data-tag="01PW">28.18.2</a> we see that for some $e \geq 0$ the section $s^ e|_{X_ s} t$ extends to a global section $\tau $ of $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes me} \otimes \mathcal{N}$. Thus both $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes me} \otimes \mathcal{N}$ and $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes me}$ are invertible sheaves which have global sections which do not vanish at any point of $E \cup \{ x_1, \ldots , x_ n\} $. Thus these are regular sections by Lemma <a href="/tag/0AYL" data-tag="0AYL">31.16.1</a>. Hence $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes me} \otimes \mathcal{N} \cong \mathcal{O}_ X(D)$ and $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes me} \cong \mathcal{O}_ X(D')$ for some effective Cartier divisors $D$ and $D'$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01X0" data-tag="01X0">31.15.10</a>. By construction $E \cap D = \emptyset $ and $E \cap D' = \emptyset $ and the proof is complete. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>